\newlabel{table:comparison}{{2}{6}{Quantitative comparison with the state-of-the-art methods for \textbf  {PPG-to-ECG translation}. RDDM consistently outperforms prior works by a large margin in all the setups. Moreover, RDDM also outperforms the best DDPM which uses $50$ sampling steps compared to ours using $10$. }{}{}}
\newlabel{table:heart-rate}{{3}{6}{Evaluation of \textbf  {Heart rate estimation.} RDDM outperforms the prior works by a significant margin.}{}{}}
\newlabel{table:afib}{{4}{6}{Evaluation of \textbf  {AFib detection} on MIMIC subset. RDDM significantly outperforms prior works and reports an F1 score close to the upper bound. $^*$re-implementation by us.}{}{}}
\newlabel{fig:qualitative}{{2}{7}{ \textbf  {Qualitative comparison} on generated ECG using varying nature of input PPG. RDDM consistently generates ECG that closely resembles the original ECG, even when the input PPG is noisy (e.g., WESAD), unlike CardioGAN and DDPM. }{}{}}
\newlabel{fig:supp-stress}{{3}{7}{ RDDM-generated ECG signals of subjects from the WESAD dataset experiencing different affective states. }{}{}}
\newlabel{fig:supp-afib}{{4}{7}{RDDM-generated ECG signals of $3$ AFib patients from the test set of MIMIC-AFib dataset.}{}{}}
\newlabel{table:wesad}{{5}{7}{Evaluation of \textbf  {Stress and Affect detection} on WESAD dataset. RDDM not only outperforms prior works, also achieves a very close performance to the upper bound.}{}{}}
\newlabel{tab:diabetes}{{6}{7}{Evaluation of \textbf  {Diabetes detection} on PPG-BP dataset. RDDM outperforms both uni-modal (Orig. PPG) and multimodal (Gen. ECG + Orig. PPG) prior works. $^{*}$re-implementation by us. $^{**}$uses pre-trained weights.}{}{}}
